WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.398
Allow-list validation defines what is acceptable and rejects everything else.

00:00:04.398 --> 00:00:08.032
For many fields, this is the strongest approach.

00:00:08.032 --> 00:00:15.107
If a status can only be pending, approved, or rejected, the application should allow only those values.

00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:22.183
If a field should be a positive integer below a known maximum, the validation should enforce that.

00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:27.346
The rule is grounded in expected behavior rather than known attack strings.

00:00:27.346 --> 00:00:30.980
Deny-list validation tries to block specific bad patterns.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:42.263
Deny lists can be useful as supporting controls, such as blocking obviously dangerous file extensions or known abusive inputs, but they are usually weaker as the primary defense.

00:00:42.263 --> 00:00:50.103
Attackers and integration bugs can produce new, encoded, partial, or unexpected forms that the deny list did not anticipate.

00:00:50.103 --> 00:00:53.737
Brittle pattern matching is a common failure mode.

00:00:53.737 --> 00:01:01.577
A regular expression that attempts to detect every dangerous string can become complex, incomplete, and hard to reason about.

00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:07.123
The better question is often simpler: what should this field contain at all?

00:01:07.123 --> 00:01:10.756
An email field should be an email-shaped value.

00:01:10.756 --> 00:01:14.772
A country field should come from known country codes.

00:01:14.772 --> 00:01:19.171
A quantity should be a number in an allowed range.

00:01:19.171 --> 00:01:22.039
Allow lists are not always trivial.

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:27.585
Some inputs, such as names, comments, search text, and international addresses, need flexibility.

00:01:27.585 --> 00:01:35.043
In those cases, validation should still define length, encoding, structure, and where the data may be used safely.

00:01:35.043 --> 00:01:42.119
When broad input is legitimate, pair validation with safe storage, safe APIs, output encoding, and context-aware handling.
